# ---- HOST-NATIVE STATION (de-bridged 2026-08-17, VICE wave #6) ----
# VICE 3.10.0's x128 runs ON THE HOST, headless: no QEMU, no guest Debian, no
# X server, no kiosk, and no in-guest helper script. Frames VICE_SHM_PATH ->
# SH_CAPTURE=shm; keys the vicectl socket + the ONE shared keysym table (VICE
# resolves keysym -> matrix through C128/gtk3_sym.vkm, the same file the
# bridged kiosk used, which is why the character behaviour — the alt key set
# included — carries over by construction); audio -sounddev wav -> named FIFO
# with the daemon as clock. Launcher: the shared
# stations/vice-native/x11-runtime.sh, VICE_NATIVE_* knobs.
# Build: scripts/build-guests/emulators/build-vice-native.sh c128.
SH_RESET_MODE=relaunch
SH_FIXTURE_DESC=Commodore 128 (PAL) at its own untouched 80-column power-on screen — "COMMODORE BASIC V7.0 122365 BYTES FREE / (C)1986 COMMODORE ELECTRONICS, LTD. / (C)1977 MICROSOFT CORP. / ALL RIGHTS RESERVED / READY." in the VDC's cyan on black, drawn by host-native VICE 3.10.0 x128. PUBLISHED SURFACE 856x576, MEASURED off the shm mapping (1 972 288 B = 64 + 856*576*4) — VICE has no MAME_SHM_SIZE equivalent, the surface IS the emulated screen, so this geometry follows from VICE_NATIVE_ARGS and VICE_NATIVE_SHM_CHIP and nothing else. NATIVE SIZE, no -VDCdsize: the bridged kiosk drew the VDC 1:1 in a 789x576 window that filled an 800x600 X root, and host-native there is no root at all, so the emulated screen simply IS the stream — the same glyph size the visitor has always seen, with the black margin gone and a quarter of the encode cost of the doubled 1712x1152 surface. The CP/M 3.0 system disk sits in drive 8 UNBOOTED; the visitor reaches CP/M by typing BOOT, which the SPA's c128 on-screen keyboard sends from one labelled button. No C64 button: GO64 works but paints the VIC-II, which is not the canvas being published. Keyboard-only exhibit; no pointer device; SID sound -> FIFO.
VICE_NATIVE_BIN=/data/vms/streamhost/assets/c128/vice-native/bin/x128
VICE_NATIVE_DATA=/data/vms/streamhost/assets/c128/vice-native/share/vice
# The ONLY thing that decides the published geometry, together with the chip
# selector below (see SH_FIXTURE_DESC). No -remotemonitor: VICE's TEXT monitor
# is dead in a headless build, and this station needs no monitor at all —
# restore-at-startup runs from -moncommands, which the launcher writes.
VICE_NATIVE_ARGS=-pal -80col
SH_VICECTL_SOCK=/data/vms/streamhost/stations/c128/ctl.sock
SH_VICESOCK_KEYMAP=/data/vms/streamhost/stations/c128/us-layout.keysyms
SH_AUDIO_SOURCE=fifo
SH_AUDIO_FIFO=/data/vms/streamhost/stations/c128/audio.fifo

# ---- THE CANVAS SELECTOR: THE ONE KNOB THIS STATION CANNOT GET WRONG ----
# x128 creates TWO canvases, VICII and VDC, and only one may publish into the
# mapping. `-80col` decides which screen the MACHINE boots into; it does NOT
# decide which canvas refreshes first. MEASURED 2026-08-17 on the pinned binary:
# with no selector the VICII claims the mapping and the station would have
# shipped the 40-column screen at 768x544 — the wrong machine's face, under the
# right flags. VDC is 856x576 and is this exhibit. A name matching no chip
# publishes NOTHING, which the boot gate reads as an empty mapping: loud, not
# quietly wrong.
VICE_NATIVE_SHM_CHIP=VDC

# ---- CHECKPOINT: ON, and it is also what dodges the drive-8 autoboot ----
# The VDC is CRTC-FAMILY but lives in src/vdc with its own snapshot module, so
# the 2026-08-17 src/crtc canvas-growth fix did not cover it and nothing could
# be assumed. TESTED EXPLICITLY on a namespaced rig before this fixture existed:
# a golden baked through the monitor's own `dump`, restored in a FRESH process,
# comes back 856x576 with 8 719 lit and 11 263 non-dominant pixels — the
# cold-boot gate's own numbers, to the pixel — the scene is pixel-correct, and
# the machine is alive at 150 M cycles with nothing printed into BASIC.
#
# The restore is also the whole disk story. A snapshot stores drive 8's STATE
# but not its MEDIA, and the C128 KERNAL boots any CP/M disk it finds in drive 8
# AT RESET — so `-8 <d64>` on the command line would autoboot CP/M and this
# station's scene is the BASIC prompt. The bridged kiosk dodged that with
# c128-attach-cpm.sh inside the guest, which waited ~10 s and spoke VICE's text
# monitor over TCP. Here the launcher attaches the disk in the SAME monitor
# command block as the undump, at the READY breakpoint, which is already past
# the boot-sector check: synchronous, no helper, nothing to race.
VICE_NATIVE_ATTACH8=/data/vms/streamhost/assets/c128/vice-native/media/cpm.d64

# ---- STANDBY (instant-ready: an emulator nobody watches costs ~0 CPU) ----
# The launcher freezes the emulator once the scene has painted; the daemon owns
# it after that (SIGSTOP after the idle grace with zero sessions, unconditional
# SIGCONT the moment a visitor connects). WITHOUT THESE TWO LINES A DE-BRIDGED
# STATION BURNS A FULL CORE AROUND THE CLOCK. _PROC_MATCH is the anti-footgun:
# the signal only ever reaches a pid whose cmdline carries THIS station's own
# asset path. Rollback is one line: SH_IDLE_PAUSE_SECS=0.
SH_IDLE_PAUSE_SECS=60
SH_IDLE_PAUSE_PIDFILE=/data/vms/streamhost/stations/c128/vice.pid
SH_IDLE_PAUSE_PROC_MATCH=assets/c128/vice-native
VICE_NATIVE_STANDBY_DELAY_S=8

# ---- KEYBOARD PACING ----
# 60/60, the VICE wave's floor, applied fleet-wide on 2026-08-17. The 80/80 the
# wave shipped on came from the vic20 bisect against THIS engine; that bisect
# was RE-RUN with realistic OVERLAPPING bursts once the exclusive-scan deadlock
# was fixed, and 60/60 was clean 12 of 12 while 40/40 corrupted 6 of 12. 60/60
# is one clear step above the last pacing observed to fail and is 28 % more
# throughput than 80/80. Verified on this station through the REAL SPA in a
# real browser, not just the control socket — a rig burst cannot see the daemon
# path or the first-visit standby race.
SH_KEY_MIN_HOLD_MS=60
SH_KEY_MIN_GAP_MS=60
# No SH_KEY_MAP and no per-station keymap file: VICE's own symbolic keymap does
# that job. The C128's own extra keys — the numeric keypad, HELP, 40/80 DISPLAY,
# ESC — stay on the SPA keyboard's overflow row exactly as before.
